=========================================================
CS 4760 Assignment 6 - Memory Management
=========================================================

How to compile:
  make

How to run:
  ./oss -n 5 -s 5 -t 3 -i 0.1 -f oss.log

Options:
  -h            Print help and exit
  -n proc       Total processes to launch (default 18)
  -s simul      Max simultaneous processes (default 18, hard cap 18)
  -t timeLim    Max simulated seconds a child exists (default 5)
  -i fraction   Min fraction-of-second between launches (default 0.5)
  -f logfile    Log file name (default oss.log)

Example:
  ./oss -n 3 -s 2 -t 4 -i 0.6 -f log.txt

=========================================================
Design Decisions
=========================================================

IPC Keys
--------
Both oss and user_proc derive IPC keys using ftok("oss.c", 1)
for shared memory and ftok("oss.c", 2) for the message queue.
Using the source file as the ftok path guarantees unique keys
per user on the shared opsys server, since each user's oss.c
has a different inode number.

Memory Layout
-------------
- Total system memory: 64K
- Frame size: 1K
- Total frames: 64
- Pages per process: 16 (each process has 16K address space)
- Page size: 1K

Each process has a page table of 16 entries. Each entry is
either -1 (page not in memory) or the frame number where
that page is currently loaded.

Frame Table
-----------
64 frame descriptors, each containing:
  - occupied: 1 if frame is in use, 0 if free
  - dirty:    1 if frame has been written to, 0 if clean
  - pid:      PID of the process owning this frame
  - page:     which page of that process is in this frame

FIFO Page Replacement
---------------------
When a page fault occurs and no free frame is available,
the oldest frame (first in, first out) is evicted. A queue
tracks the order frames were loaded. The evicted frame's
owner has its page table entry cleared. If the evicted
frame has its dirty bit set, an additional 14ms is added
to the clock to simulate writing the dirty page to disk
before loading the new page.

Page Fault Handling
-------------------
When a page fault occurs:
  1. OSS finds a free frame or evicts via FIFO
  2. The new page is loaded into the frame
  3. The process is blocked for 14ms (disk I/O time)
  4. OSS does NOT send a reply message — process stays
     blocked in msgrcv until the I/O time has elapsed
  5. OSS checks blocked processes each iteration and
     sends the grant message when the time is reached

If all processes are blocked simultaneously, OSS advances
the clock directly to the earliest unblock time to avoid
spinning indefinitely (soft deadlock prevention).

Page Hit Handling
-----------------
When a requested page is already in a frame:
  - Clock advances by 100ns
  - If write: dirty bit is set on the frame
  - Grant message sent immediately

Message Protocol
----------------
oss -> user_proc:  address == 1  your turn
                   address == 0  terminate now

user_proc -> oss:  address >= 0  memory request
                   rwFlag  == 0  read
                   rwFlag  == 1  write
                   address == -1 terminating

Memory addresses are generated randomly in range
0 to (16 * 1024 - 1). Page is extracted by dividing
address by 1024. Bias is 80% reads, 20% writes.

Termination
-----------
oss stops after opt_n processes launched or 5 real-time
seconds (SIGALRM). Uses sigaction with no SA_RESTART so
msgrcv() is interrupted by the alarm. On SIGINT/SIGTERM
all children are killed and IPC is cleaned up.

Log Output
----------
All output goes to both stdout and the log file. Log
writing stops at 10,000 lines. Every 500ms simulated
time the process table is printed. Every 1 second the
full memory map (frame table + page tables) is printed.

Statistics (final report)
--------------------------
  - Total processes launched
  - Total memory reads
  - Total memory writes
  - Total page faults
  - Page fault percentage

=========================================================
Generative AI Use
=========================================================

Generative AI used: Claude (Anthropic, claude.ai)

Prompts (paraphrased):
  1. Build Project 6 incrementally starting from Project 5 base
  2. Set up shared.h with frame table and page table structures
  3. Add main loop with forking and basic memory request logging
  4. Add page table and frame table logic with FIFO replacement
  5. Add dirty bit tracking and page fault blocking
  6. Add all-blocked detection to prevent soft deadlock

Summary: Claude was used to build the project incrementally,
verifying each step before moving to the next. The most
valuable contributions were the FIFO queue implementation,
the page fault blocking mechanism, and the soft deadlock
prevention when all processes are simultaneously blocked
waiting on disk I/O.